speechtext:
Agreed.

[Editor's note: The answer referred to is as follows:1
The national harbours board advises as follows:
1.	The Montreal harbour planning committee is at present engaged in a study of Montreal harbour and its facilities, and its terms of reference do not include Sorel.
2.	The members of this committee are: Paul Kuhring, former chief engineer of the St. Lawrence ship channel branch of the Department of Transport; chairman of the committee; G. H. Kohl, alternate member, St. Lawrence river joint board of engineers, Canadian section; D. F. Purves, chief of development branch, research department, Canadian National Railways; M. Boudreau, assistant chief engineer, St. Lawrence river ship channel branch of the Department of Transport; G. S. Anderson, assistant port manager (administration), Montreal harbour.
3.	Mr. Paul L. Kuhring is a graduate engineer of U. of N.B. (1913); he was employed 34 years with D.O.T. as engineer, assistant chief engineer and chief engineer of the St. Lawrence ship channel branch of D.O.T. until July 16, 1958, when he retired from the public service; now full time chairman of the committee at a salary of $10,140 per annum;
Mr. Geo. H. Kohl is a graduate engineer of McGill University (1910); he was chief engineer of the St. Lawrence river joint board of engineers; is now alternate representative for Canada on this joint board on a per diem allowance when acting as such; is a member of the committee at a per diem allowance of $100 when performing such duties;
Mr. Purves is a graduate in commerce from U.B.C. and holds a master's degree in transportation, industrial engineering and economics (Columbia University); he is chief of the development branch, research department, C.N.R., and does not receive any additional remuneration as a member of the Montreal harbour planning committee;
J. Maurice G. Boudreau is a graduate engineer, University of Montreal (1931), and has been employed with the St. Lawrence ship channel branch of D.O.T. since 1935, of which branch he was promoted as assistant chief engineer in 1958; he is still acting in this capacity and does not receive any additional remuneration as a member of the Montreal harbour planning committee;
G. S. Anderson is a graduate engineer U. of Sask. (1931), has been on the staff of the National harbours board since 1936; he was promoted to assistant port manager (admin).

Montreal harbour, in 1957, and is still employed as such; he does not receive any additional remuneration as a member of the Montreal harbour planning committee.
